// The thread kind's step (thread.kind): owed by the watcher, which coordd answers itself, as it does the clerk's. It
// runs `sort` below once, statelessly, with the model policy names, and answers `intake` with its verdict; a call
// that fails, or answers out of shape, is answered OneOff. OPEN is coordd's: the open tickets TO takes part in, one a
// line, with its kind and title. Unsure(tickets) (paper-research m1249) is for words that could go on more than one of
// them: the watcher does not guess, the owner picks (two or more tickets, each open, or coordd answers OneOff).

call intake(text: Text, to: Agent) -> Thread | Place(ticket: Ticket) | OneOff | Unsure(tickets: [Ticket])
  task intake

call sort(text: Text, to: Agent, open: Text) -> Thread | Place(ticket: Ticket) | OneOff | Unsure(tickets: [Ticket])
  task intake
  template
    The owner of a software team wrote the message below to {{ to }}, one of the team's agents. Say what it is:

    - OneOff: a question or a remark for {{ to }} alone, to be answered and forgotten ("what is 5+5", "how are you
      getting on", "show me the last commit"). Most short messages are this.
    - Place(ticket): it continues the subject of one of the open tickets listed after it: it answers or follows up
      what was said there, or is about the same piece of work. Sharing a file, a module or the agent is not enough; a
      different question about the same code is a Thread or a OneOff. A short answer ("yes", "go ahead", "looks good",
      "no, keep it") belongs on the ticket whose last post waits on the owner's answer, when exactly one does. Give the
      ticket's id as listed. A reply to a ticket that asks the owner whom a post is for belongs on that ticket:
      words that say whom ("for lead", "fixer should"), or the same message said again more clearly.
    - Unsure(tickets): it could go on more than one of the open tickets, and the words do not say which. The plain
      case is a short answer while two or more tickets wait on the owner's answer. Give every ticket it could go on;
      the owner will pick. Do not guess between them.
    - Thread: it starts a piece of work or a discussion worth keeping, which none of the open tickets is about.

    When unsure between OneOff and Thread, say OneOff: the owner can make it a thread later.

    The message:
    <<<
    {{ text }}
    >>>

    The open tickets {{ to }} takes part in:
    {{ open }}
